# Workflow

## BEFORE you start work
- Use `veans list --ready` to find tasks you can claim right now. The
  server decides: Todo, unassigned, no unfinished blocker, and none of the
  task's owned paths leased by another in-progress task. `veans ready`
  shows every Todo task with the reasons it is NOT ready (`assigned`,
  `blocked` + `blocked_by`, `lease_conflict` + `lease_conflicts`).
- Read the task's scope before touching code: `veans show {{ .TaskIDExample }}` includes
  `scope` — `paths_owned` (files you will edit; leased to you on claim),
  `paths_affected` (read/depend on, do not edit), `endpoints`, `notes`.
  **Stay inside `paths_owned`.** If the work needs a file outside it, widen
  the scope first (`veans scope {{ .TaskIDExample }} --paths-owned ... --paths-owned <new>`);
  the server refuses with `CONFLICT` if another task holds it — then split
  the work into a new task instead of editing that file.
- **Start from current code.** Before you claim anything, fetch and get on top
  of the integration branch:
  ```
  git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main     # or: git pull --rebase
  ```
  A session that starts on a week-old checkout writes a week-old diff. You will
  pass your own gates, open the pull request, and only then find that the file
  you edited moved under you — and the further in you are, the more expensive
  that is. Thirty seconds here saves an hour of rebasing later.
- If a task already exists, claim it: `veans claim {{ .TaskIDExample }}`
  The claim is atomic and exclusive: if another agent already holds the
  task, it has left Todo since you listed it, it is blocked, or one of its
  owned paths is leased, the command fails with `CONFLICT` — pick a
  different task, do NOT retry the same one. Claiming a task you already
  hold is a harmless no-op.
- Otherwise, create one and start it in one step, with its scope:
  `veans create "<short title>" -s in-progress -d "<short description>" --paths-owned pkg/foo/** --endpoint "POST /api/v2/foo"`
  then claim it so the paths are leased: `veans claim {{ .TaskIDExample }}`
- `veans leases` lists what every in-progress task is editing right now.
- Paths are relative to this repository. In a project that spans several
  repositories they carry a `repo:` prefix (`api:pkg/models/**`); when
  `.veans.yml` sets `repository`, bare paths you pass are prefixed for you.

## WHILE you work
- Keep the task's description in sync with what you're doing. Use HTML
  (not markdown) — see the "Description format" section below for why
  and the canonical TipTap shapes Vikunja's web UI renders nicely:
  `veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} --description-append '<ul data-type="taskList"><li data-type="taskItem" data-checked="false"><p>step 1</p></li></ul>'`
- For surgical edits, prefer `--description-replace-old` /
  `--description-replace-new`. To check off a task item, replace
  `data-checked="false"><p>step 1</p>` with `data-checked="true"><p>step 1</p>`
  (errors if the old text isn't unique — same semantics as the Edit tool).
- Post a comment on significant decisions, discoveries, or course-changes:
  `veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} --comment '<p>Discovered Y; pivoting to Z because …</p>'`
- For sub-work that could be assigned separately, create real subtasks
  via `--parent`. For incremental check-off lists, use task-list items
  in the description instead.

## BEFORE you open a PR
- Run `veans check`. It diffs your branch against main, reads the `Refs:`
  trailers from your commits and asks the server whether every changed
  file is inside your tasks' `paths_owned` and outside every other task's
  lease. `CONFLICT` means a stray or a collision: widen your scope
  (`veans scope`), split the extra work into a new task, or revert the
  file. The PR's scope check runs the same query and will fail otherwise.
- Working for a long time without touching the board? `veans heartbeat {{ .TaskIDExample }}`
  keeps your leases from being flagged stale (every update, comment and
  claim already counts as activity). A stale lease notifies your owner;
  if the server is configured to auto-release, silent leases are dropped
  and the task gets a comment saying so — claim again before continuing.
- `veans hooks install` (once per checkout) makes git run `veans check
  --staged` before every commit, so a stray never reaches a branch.

## AFTER you finish work
- Move to `in-review` and post a summary comment. **Never close tasks
  yourself** — merging the PR closes them: the Workman merge hook reads the
  `Refs:` trailer on your commits and marks those tasks done.
  ```
  veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} -s in-review \
    --comment '<h3>Summary of Changes</h3><ul><li>first thing</li><li>second thing</li></ul>'
  ```
- **Never approve or merge a pull request, yours or anyone else's.** Landing
  work is a human's decision. Open it, enable auto-merge if you like — it
  cannot land without a human's approval — and leave it.
- **Then start the next ticket. Do not wait for CI, and do not wait for the
  merge.** Once the PR is open and the task is in review, the work is handed
  over: a human enables auto-merge and GitHub merges it when the checks go
  green, and the merge hook closes the task from the `Refs:` trailer without
  you. Sitting and watching a ten-minute test run is ten minutes nobody asked
  you to spend.
- Two things to know before you walk away, because they are what turns
  "handed over" into "stalled":
  - `in-review` does NOT release your leases. If the next ticket you want
    overlaps the files you just worked on, it will be refused — that is the
    lease doing its job. Pick a ticket that does not overlap, or wait for the
    merge after all.
  - If CI fails on your PR after you have moved on, the task comes back to
    you. Check the PRs you have open before claiming a third or fourth ticket;
    a queue of red PRs behind you is worse than having waited.
- If you abandon work, scrap the task with a reason:
  `veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} -s scrapped --reason "obsolete: <why>"`
- Leases are released when the task is done or scrapped. If you stop
  working on a task without finishing it, release its paths so others can
  proceed: `veans release {{ .TaskIDExample }}` (status and assignees stay).
- If you are NOT going to do the task at all — claimed in error, blocked by
  something you cannot move, handing off — use
  `veans unclaim {{ .TaskIDExample }}` instead. Moving the bucket back and
  releasing leases is NOT enough: a task with an assignee is never ready,
  whatever bucket it sits in and whatever you wrote on the card, so a
  half-handed-back ticket is one nobody can pick up.

## Commit messages
Include the task identifier on a `Refs:` line so the merge hook can close
tasks automatically when the PR lands:

```
fix: handle empty project identifiers

Refs: {{ .TaskIDExample }}
```

# Description format

**Descriptions and comments are HTML, not markdown.** Vikunja's web UI
renders them through the TipTap rich-text editor, which interprets the
stored field as HTML. Plain markdown saves as literal text and shows up
ugly in the UI; write HTML directly. Tokens are cheap, conversion bugs
aren't.

**Titles are plaintext — not HTML, not markdown.** They show up in
list views, breadcrumbs, and notification subjects; tags would leak
through as `&lt;p&gt;…&lt;/p&gt;` everywhere. Write
`fix the bug` not `<p>fix the bug</p>` or `**fix** the bug`.

**Write so a smart fifteen-year-old could follow it.** Descriptions,
comments and titles are read by people catching up on work they did not do,
sometimes weeks later, sometimes on a phone. Aim for the level of a good
newspaper: short sentences, ordinary words, one idea at a time.

- Say what changed and why it matters before any detail of how.
- Prefer the plain word: "stops", not "precludes"; "we cannot tell", not
  "it is indeterminate"; "the file two people are editing", not "the
  contended resource".
- Expand a name the first time you use it. `paths_owned` means nothing to
  someone who has not read the docs — "the files this task will edit
  (`paths_owned`)" costs six words and saves a lookup.
- Keep jargon you cannot avoid, and explain it once. Do not strip out
  precision to sound simple: "a merge conflict is certain" is both plain
  AND exact. "There may be issues" is neither.
- A comment nobody can act on is a comment nobody needed. End with what
  happens next, or what someone should do.

Bad:  <p>Refactored the scope-check ingress to canonicalise at the
      boundary, eliminating dialect divergence.</p>
Good: <p>Scope paths are now written one way everywhere. Before this, the
      same file could be listed two different ways and the two would not
      recognise each other, so two people could edit it at once without
      either being warned.</p>

Canonical TipTap shapes the web UI renders cleanly:

```html
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>Two short paragraphs explaining the task.</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>
<ul data-type="taskList">
  <li data-type="taskItem" data-checked="false"><p>find the bug</p></li>
  <li data-type="taskItem" data-checked="false"><p>write the fix</p></li>
  <li data-type="taskItem" data-checked="true"><p>write the test</p></li>
</ul>

<h3>Notes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>regular bullet point</li>
  <li>another one</li>
</ul>

<p>Inline <code>code</code>, <strong>bold</strong>, <em>italic</em>,
and a <a href="https://example.com">link</a>.</p>

<pre><code class="language-go">if err != nil { return err }</code></pre>

<blockquote><p>A quote, e.g. from a linked issue.</p></blockquote>

<hr>
```

Important details:
- Task-list items use `<ul data-type="taskList">` and
  `<li data-type="taskItem" data-checked="true|false">` — that's what
  gives users an interactive checkbox. Plain `<ul><li>` lists render as
  static bullets.
- Inner text of task items goes inside `<p>` — the editor expects block
  content in the `<li>`.
- Don't add `data-task-id` attributes manually; the editor auto-fills
  them on first save.
- Escape `<`, `>`, `&` when they appear in literal text (`&lt;`, `&gt;`,
  `&amp;`). Inside `<pre><code>` blocks you only need to escape these
  three; line breaks and indentation are preserved.
- `--description-replace-old/new` matches raw HTML byte-for-byte. Make
  the `old` string unique (include surrounding tags to disambiguate).

# Output

Every `list`, `show`, `create`, `update`, `claim`, `api` call emits JSON
on stdout — no flag needed, no human-formatted variant. Errors land on
stderr as `{"code":"...","error":"..."}` with non-zero exit; branch on
`code` (NOT_FOUND, CONFLICT, VALIDATION_ERROR, AUTH_ERROR,
RATE_LIMITED, NOT_CONFIGURED, BOT_USERS_UNAVAILABLE, UNKNOWN).

`show`/`create`/`update`/`claim` return a single task; `list` returns
an array. Useful fields:

- `id` (numeric, internal) — pass to `api` calls
- `index` (per-project) — what `{{ .TaskIDExample }}` resolves to
- `done`, `priority`, `title`, `description` (HTML)
- `buckets[]` — current bucket per view; match `project_view_id` to
  yours from `.veans.yml` and read `id` to know the status
- `assignees[]`, `labels[]` — `[]` if absent
- `scope` — `{paths_owned[], paths_affected[], endpoints[], notes}`;
  absent when the task has no scope
- `leases[]` — the path patterns this task currently holds; absent when none

Trust your JSON parser; we won't add new fields without notice.

# Status model

| Status        | Bucket name    | Done flag | Who moves there?                         |
| ------------- | -------------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `todo`        | Todo           | false     | created here by default                  |
| `in-progress` | In Progress    | false     | `veans claim` or `update -s in-progress` |
| `in-review`   | In Review      | false     | you, when work is finished               |
| `completed`   | Done           | true      | humans / merge hook only                 |
| `scrapped`    | Scrapped       | true      | you, with --reason                       |

# Common commands

```
veans list                         # all tasks, tree view
veans list --ready                 # ready to start, in queue order (board drag order)
veans list --ready --first         # the one task at the top of the queue — claim this
veans list --mine                  # tasks assigned to you
veans list --branch                # tasks tagged with the current git branch
veans list --filter "priority > 3"  # raw Vikunja filter expression
veans show {{ .TaskIDExample }}                  # full task detail (JSON)

veans create "title" -s in-progress -d "<p>HTML body — see Description format.</p>"
veans create "title" --label bug --priority 4 --parent {{ .TaskIDExample }}
veans create "title" --blocked-by {{ .TaskIDExample }}
veans create "title" --paths-owned pkg/models/** --paths-affected pkg/routes/** --endpoint "POST /api/v2/tasks/{id}/claim"

veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} -s in-review --comment '<p>Summary of changes…</p>'
veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} --description-append '<ul data-type="taskList"><li data-type="taskItem" data-checked="false"><p>new step</p></li></ul>'
veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} --description-replace-old 'data-checked="false"><p>step 1</p>' --description-replace-new 'data-checked="true"><p>step 1</p>'
veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} --label-add bug --label-remove flaky
veans update {{ .TaskIDExample }} -s scrapped --reason "obsolete: replaced by {{ .TaskIDExample }}"

veans claim {{ .TaskIDExample }}                 # atomic: assign yourself + In Progress + branch label + lease paths_owned; CONFLICT if held/blocked/leased
veans claim --force {{ .TaskIDExample }}         # claim from a bucket other than Todo (never overrides another user)
veans ready                        # ready queue with reasons (assigned / blocked / lease_conflict)
veans scope {{ .TaskIDExample }}                 # show the task's scope
veans scope {{ .TaskIDExample }} --paths-owned pkg/models/** --paths-affected docs/** --endpoint "GET /api/v2/x"
veans scope {{ .TaskIDExample }} --clear         # drop the scope and its leases
veans leases                       # who is editing what right now (stale: true = holder inactive)
veans release {{ .TaskIDExample }}               # give up this task's leases without changing its status
veans unclaim {{ .TaskIDExample }}               # hand the task back entirely: assignee off, back to Todo, leases dropped, branch label removed
veans sync {{ .TaskIDExample }}                  # what main has moved under you, by severity, and the commands to catch up
veans heartbeat {{ .TaskIDExample }}             # mark your leases active during long silent work
veans check                        # changed files vs your tasks' scopes + others' leases; CONFLICT on strays
veans check --staged               # same, for the index (pre-commit)
veans plan --dry-run plan.json     # lint a decomposition (keys, cycles, overlapping paths)
veans plan plan.json               # create every task, relation and scope of a plan in one go
veans plan --export > board.json   # the open board in plan shape; new tasks may reference its keys
veans agents                       # who is working here: bots with the human behind each, and humans
veans watch [--exec CMD] [--once]  # orchestrator: one JSON line per task.ready / task.unready / lease.stale
veans hooks install                # git pre-commit hook running 'veans check --staged'
marshal mcp                        # the same tools over MCP, plus resolve_references / worktree_plan / board_health (when marshal is installed)
veans api GET /tasks/...           # escape hatch for raw REST when curated cmds don't fit
```

# Decomposing work (owner's agent)

Write the whole breakdown as one plan and let the server lint it before
anything is created:

```json
{"tasks": [
  {"key": "claim", "title": "atomic claim endpoint", "priority": 3,
   "scope": {"paths_owned": ["pkg/models/task_claim.go"], "paths_affected": ["pkg/models/tasks.go"],
             "endpoints": ["POST /api/v2/tasks/{id}/claim"], "notes": "<p>No frontend.</p>"}},
  {"key": "badges", "title": "board badges", "blocked_by": ["claim"],
   "scope": {"paths_owned": ["frontend/src/components/tasks/**"], "paths_affected": [], "endpoints": [], "notes": ""}},
  {"key": "epic", "title": "claiming", "description": "<p>Umbrella.</p>"},
  {"key": "docs", "title": "docs", "parent_key": "epic", "follows": ["badges"],
   "scope": {"paths_owned": ["docs/**"], "paths_affected": [], "endpoints": [], "notes": ""}}
]}
```

To extend an existing board, `veans plan --export` prints the open tasks
in this shape keyed by identifier; a new plan may use those keys in
`parent_key`, `blocked_by` and `follows` without redefining them.

`veans plan --dry-run plan.json` reports errors (duplicate/unknown keys,
cycles, invalid paths — nothing is created) and warnings (a task without
`paths_owned`, two tasks owning overlapping paths with no dependency
between them, overlap with an open task already on the board). Fix the
errors, decide on the warnings, then `veans plan plan.json`.

# Project quick reference

- Project ID: `{{ .ProjectID }}`{{ if .ProjectIdentifier }}, identifier: `{{ .ProjectIdentifier }}` (task IDs render as `{{ .ProjectIdentifier }}-NN`){{ else }} (no identifier; task IDs render as `#NN`){{ end }}
- Kanban view: `{{ .ViewID }}`
- Buckets: Todo=`{{ .Buckets.Todo }}`, In Progress=`{{ .Buckets.InProgress }}`, In Review=`{{ .Buckets.InReview }}`, Done=`{{ .Buckets.Done }}`, Scrapped=`{{ .Buckets.Scrapped }}`
- Label namespace: `veans:` (auto-prepended; e.g. `--label bug` becomes `veans:bug`)
- Before opening a PR, run `veans sync {{ .TaskIDExample }}`. If it reports
  OWNED collisions, the integration branch has moved inside a file you claimed
  to edit and a conflict is certain — rebase and re-run your gates first.
  AFFECTED means something you read changed: no conflict, but your assumptions
  may be stale. It prints commands and never runs them; your worktree is yours.
- `veans update -s in-review` REFUSES while you are behind in a file you own,
  and so does the PR check. That is not bureaucracy: the diff a reviewer would
  read is not the diff that will merge. Rebase, re-run the gates, then move it.
  `--force` exists for the case where you know the change is trivially
  rebasable, and posts a comment on the task saying you used it.
- Branch labels: `veans:branch:<branch-name>` — `veans claim` adds the current branch
  automatically, UNLESS you are on the repository default branch, where it adds
  nothing and warns. Branch first, or claim again after branching, or pass
  `--branch`. A label saying `veans:branch:main` tells the next reader nothing.

The agent prompt above overrides TodoWrite. Anything you would have tracked
there should live in Vikunja instead, where it stays visible across sessions
and to humans collaborating on the project.
